{
  "_doc": "Loop configuration - the machine-readable knobs the driver (scripts/run_campaign.py --supervise) reads. Set at scaffold time by new-campaign from this campaign's unit cost (the WHY lives in roles/worker.md, 'How the loop runs'); human-editable mid-campaign - the driver logs any change it detects. Knob semantics: STRUCTURED_LOOP_PROPOSAL s4.1 rules 2-4.",
  "campaign": {
    "_doc": "What KIND of campaign this is - the two properties every mechanical check branches on. Both are SETUP-TIME decisions, not per-experiment skips: turning a control off because one result looks awkward is the failure the control exists to prevent. Changing either mid-campaign is legitimate only when the campaign's nature actually changed, and the change is visible in every later snapshot manifest (results/snapshots/<exp>/manifest.txt records both), never only here. prediction_mode: what the a-priori prediction gate requires before it will spend an evaluation - 'range' (a falsifiable numeric [low, high]; the default, and the strongest control), 'direction' (which way the metric should move versus a named prior experiment, and why - no number, still mechanically checkable), or 'none' (no bound; requires prediction_mode_reason, and is honest only for a genuinely unboundable answer - a fabricated range is a fake control, which is worse than none). noise_model: 'stochastic' (repeated runs differ; a win must clear the replicate spread) or 'deterministic' (repeated runs are identical, so the comparison is exact and the relevant test is a numerical tolerance, not a spread). noise_floor: the once-estimated replicate spread for a stochastic campaign (verify/SKILL.md, 'Clear the noise floor'), re-estimated only when something that could move it changes; null = not yet estimated, which leaves the noise-floor check unavailable rather than assumed-passed. tolerance: the numerical equality tolerance for a deterministic campaign; null = not yet set. metric_version / data_version: the comparability tags stamped onto every scoreboard row and every result JSON. A ranking claim is valid ONLY WITHIN one (metric_version, data_version) pair - when the FROZEN metric or the dataset changes, BUMP the tag here and re-score saved contenders under the new version before comparing. Never compare a number across versions. reproducible: whether one unit can be independently RE-EXECUTED from its config alone (seeded; not a one-shot measurement, not hardware-in-the-loop). The verifier re-executes only eligible units - reproducible AND a measured solve time within a quarter of worker.iteration_timeout_hours, recorded per experiment in results/checks_<NN>.json under verification.re_execution - and reviews the rest from the frozen record, saying so in the verdict.",
    "prediction_mode": "{{PREDICTION_MODE}}",
    "prediction_mode_reason": "{{PREDICTION_MODE_REASON}}",
    "noise_model": "{{NOISE_MODEL}}",
    "noise_floor": {{NOISE_FLOOR}},
    "tolerance": {{METRIC_TOLERANCE}},
    "metric_version": "{{METRIC_VERSION}}",
    "data_version": "{{DATA_VERSION}}",
    "reproducible": true,
    "_doc_metric_revision": "metric_revision + metric_epochs: the FROZEN metric is epoched, not immutable. A revision of what it measures (kind 'semantics') or a fix that makes the specified metric run correctly (kind 'mechanics') opens a new metric EPOCH: bump metric_version, add an entry to metric_epochs keyed by that version - {kind, status, evidence, expected_direction, opened_by: 'worker@<exp_name>' | 'user'} - re-score retained contenders under the new ruler, and leave one line in results/JOURNAL.md Findings. metric_epochs exists only once a campaign has revised its metric: with no block, the current metric_version IS the co-designed first epoch (ratified). evidence: a semantics epoch cites the verify kill verdict against the old metric (results/verdicts/<id>.json); a mechanics epoch names the correctness check that reproduced the discrepancy. expected_direction: 'harder' | 'easier' | 'neutral' - what the revision does to the current best's score ('easier' is the goalpost-moving shape and needs a REFUTED-tier verdict). status: 'pending' until the user ratifies it, then 'ratified' or 'rolled-back' - standing never deletes a result; a rolled-back epoch's rows stay on the scoreboard, labelled by their metric_version. The block is append-only and hook-guarded; ratification is the user's, from an attended session. The runner binds each epoch's scorer hash in results/correctness_state.json on its first experiment and refuses to spend on a change to the scorer's code that no epoch declares (comments, docstrings and formatting do not count). metric_revision: WHO may open an epoch. 'cautious' (default): any revision the loop's agents want raises results/ESCALATE.md and the loop waits for the user. 'autonomous': the agents may enact a VERIFIED revision themselves as a pending epoch and continue; the user ratifies after. 'semi-autonomous' is reserved (not yet defined) and is refused. The dial is the USER's decision: it is changed from an attended session (edit this file, ask run-campaign, or `run_campaign.py --set-metric-revision`), never by a driver-spawned role - the config guard refuses that - and the driver logs every change it detects and stamps the governing value into every snapshot manifest.",
    "metric_revision": "{{METRIC_REVISION}}"
  },
  "unit_cost": {
